"""
Bayesian A/B Testing with Beta-Binomial Posteriors — is B really better, or is that luck?

WHAT IT DOES: You ran an A/B experiment (e.g. two page designs): A converted 60/100,
B converted 65/100. This computes P(B > A) — the probability that B's true rate beats
A's — using Beta posteriors, plus the posterior mean rate of each variant. Uses a
Gaussian approximation to the Beta for sampling efficiency.

WHEN TO USE: You want a probabilistic answer to "is B better than A?" with uncertainty
quantification, especially for small sample sizes. Unlike a frequentist p-value, this
gives you a direct probability statement: "there's a 76% chance B is truly better."

GUARANTEES: P(B > A) > 0.5 when B's observed rate exceeds A's. Posterior means reflect
the data (mean_b > mean_a when B's observed rate is higher). With moderate sample sizes
and a planted difference, P(B > A) exceeds 0.7.

NOT: A robust A/B test. The shipped version has NO input validation — successes > trials
or zero trials produce silent confident fiction (AURA-E004, E005). The retrofit adds
proper guards. Also uses a Gaussian approximation to the Beta, not exact Beta sampling.
"""
